    "summary": "High‑energy Warfield ’82 with monster Scarlet>Fire and jazzy Estimated Prophet",
    "review": "The February 17 1982 Warfield show is a pure burst of Dead energy that still feels fresh decades later. The band launches the night with a rousing New Minglewood Blues → Sugaree opener and builds momentum through Bird Song, C.C. Rider and Tennessee Jed before diving into a second‑set firestorm. Scarlet Begonias segues straight into an unforgettable Fire on the Mountain jam – Brent Mydland’s quirky vocal/keyboard phrase shines here – and rolls right into a jazzy Estimated Prophet that features Bob Weir’s playful scatting and a deep “Phil zone” bass surge. Eyes of the World, The Wheel (complete with a rare pre‑Wheel “Tiger” tease) and a swinging Sugar Magnolia round out a set that never loses its drive.\n\nWhile several recordings exist, the Miller soundboard (gd1982-02-17.sbd.miller.112408.flac16) captures the performance with bright tonal balance and enough clarity to let every improvisational twist breathe, despite the era‑typical low‑end roll‑off and a few tape‑flip cuts. For listeners who want the cleanest full‑show experience, this is the go‑to source; the patched Warner SBD is also solid but leans harsh on the highs, and audience tapes add raw ambience at the cost of fidelity.",
